#---
# purpose: "a closure chosen by a runtime branch reaches the forwarder as a union of two constructions, and the word it carries decides which body runs"
# oracle: oracle.exs
#---
#
# fz-kdt.127, the dynamic half. No key can pin which closure `f` is: the
# `case` scrutinee comes back out of the mailbox, so `f` is typed as the union
# `#lambda(int) | #lambda(float)` and the only thing that can separate the two
# is the word the mint stamped on the value.
#
# No activation key can statically choose between those two arrivals. The
# artifact therefore keeps a nonempty two-arm runtime construction dispatch:
# its disjoint callable predicates name the same lambda at `int` and `float`
# capture layouts and select the corresponding `P.run/2` specialization. The
# construction word minted on the closure is what answers that question.
#
# The twin `same_lambda_two_capture_types` is the static control: retaining the
# int-vs-float capture tuple separates its keys, so its callable calls are
# statically grounded and ask no runtime question either.
defmodule P do
  def twice(f, x), do: f.(f.(x))
  def run(f, x), do: twice(f, x)
  def mk(c), do: fn (n) -> n + c end
end

def main() do
  send(self(), 1)
  n = receive do v -> v end
  f = case n > 0 do
    true -> P.mk(1)
    _ -> P.mk(0.5)
  end
  dbg(P.run(f, 10))
  g = case n < 0 do
    true -> P.mk(1)
    _ -> P.mk(0.5)
  end
  dbg(P.run(g, 10))
end
